"""Original-output explanations composed against the committed Unit18 contract."""
A={
'A01-O001':'''The king gave the book to the man. **Se cyning** is the nominative giver, **ðǣm męnn** the dative singular recipient, and **ðā bōc** the accusative singular gift. The article and giving frame identify męnn as singular dative here; its resemblance to a plural form does not make several recipients.''',
'A01-O002':'''**Hīe** is feminine accusative singular, referring to **bōc**, the book read. Grammatical gender allows the same pronoun to refer to an inanimate feminine noun; “it” is the natural English rendering. Reading it as “they” would also conflict with its object role here.''',
'A01-O003':'''Either the king or the man can be the reader. Both are masculine singular and can be resumed by nominative **hē**, the subject of **rǣdde**. The stated absence of further context leaves that choice unresolved. Neither proximity to męnn nor continuity with the earlier subject is a grammatical requirement.''',
'A01-O004':'''The man is now the explicitly named nominative subject; **hīe** still refers to the book as the accusative object. The result means “The man read it.” A grammatical rearrangement may preserve those roles, but retaining hē alone leaves the ambiguity that the task asks you to remove.''',
'A02-O001':'''**Ic** is the king, the speaker inside the quotation. **Geseah** presents his seeing as earlier than his quoted statement: “I saw the man.” It does not refer to the narrator who introduces the quotation.''',
'A02-O002':'''“The king said that he had seen the man.” **Sægde** introduces reported content; **hē** keeps the king as the seer, and **gesāwe** is preterite subjunctive third singular. **Ðone mann** remains the accusative person seen. Repeating se cyning inside the clause can make the reference explicit. An indicative report can be valid language, but does not meet this task’s requested subjunctive.''',
'A02-O003':'''No. Here the subjunctive presents the reported content and does not establish its truth or falsity. The seeing is earlier because of the original statement’s temporal viewpoint, not because every preterite subjunctive must express an earlier event or a false proposition.''',
'A03-O001':'''“The men who saw the king sang.” “The men that saw the king sang” has the same meaning. The men perform both actions; the king is the person seen, not a seer or the singer.''',
'A03-O002':'''**Ðe** represents the men as the subject of **gesāwon**, preterite indicative third plural. The main subject **ðā męnn** governs **sungon**, also preterite indicative third plural. **Ðone cyning** is accusative singular inside the relative clause and controls neither verb’s number.''',
'A03-O003':'''“The man who saw the king sang.” **Se mann** replaces the plural subject; **geseah** and **sang** are third singular. The object **ðone cyning** stays singular. Changing only one finite verb would leave the other action attached to an unrequested plural subject.''',
'C01-O001':'''The man is the main nominative subject of **sealde**. Inside the relative, **se cyning** is the subject of **geseah**, and invariant **ðe** represents the man as its accusative object. **Ðǣm cnihte** is the servant receiving the accusative book. Word order may vary while those roles remain clear; making the king give the book changes the meaning.''',
'C02-O001':'''The king commanded the men to give the servant the books. **Hēt** has accusative **ðā męnn** as the instructed actors of plain infinitive **sęllan**. The servant is the dative recipient and **ðā bēc** the accusative plural gift. Although męnn has the same spelling in nominative and accusative plural, it is accusative in this construction. A finite plural verb in place of sęllan misses the requested infinitive frame.''',
'F01-O001':'''**Se assa** is nominative singular; **ðone sacc** is the accusative singular possession. **Hæfð** agrees with the one ass. The weak noun’s nominative assa must not be replaced by oblique singular assan.''',
'F01-O002':'''**Ðā assan** is weak nominative plural; **ðā saccas** is strong accusative plural. **Habbað** agrees with the plural possessors. The requested two noun patterns differ: an invented strong plural of assa would miss its paradigm.''',
'F01-O003':'''The men are the nominative plural givers and govern **sęllað**. **Ðǣm assum** is dative plural, the recipients; **hwǣte** is the accusative mass singular thing given. Grammatical changes of object order are possible. Making the asses the things given, or inventing a count plural of wheat, changes the required relations.''',
'F01-O004':'''The mass singular **se hwǣte** governs **is**. **On ðǣm saccum** gives a static location, hence dative plural. The several sacks do not turn the wheat into a plural subject or require a plural copula.''',
'F01-O005':'''In the plural sentence, **assan** has the weak nominative plural ending **-an**, whereas **saccas** has strong accusative plural **-as**. Both classes have dative plural **-um**: **assum** as recipients and **saccum** after static on. Wheat remains mass singular. A shared dative ending does not make all the forms of the two declensions identical.''',
'F02-O001':'''**Beðearf** is present indicative third singular, agreeing with the man. The selected need frame governs genitive singular **hwǣtes**. English places “wheat” directly after “needs,” but that does not determine its Old English case.''',
'F02-O002':'''**Beðorfton** is preterite indicative third plural: the men needed wheat. **Hwǣtes** remains genitive mass singular. Present plural **beðurfon** would give the right subject number but the wrong time.''',
'F02-O003':'''**Beðearf** is present indicative third singular; **beðorfton** is preterite indicative third plural. **Hwǣtes** is genitive singular in both because beðurfan governs the thing needed in this selected frame. Changing the subject’s number does not change the mass noun’s number or its government. Other attested government does not meet this exercise’s genitive target.''',
'F03-O001':'''**Oncnāwe** is present indicative first singular. **Ðone cyning** is the singular accusative person recognized. The long ā belongs to this present stem; a past form would change “recognize” to “recognized.”''',
'F03-O002':'''**Oncnēowon** is preterite indicative first plural, with the past **cnēow-** stem. Only the recognizers change number; **ðone cyning** remains one accusative person. Pluralizing the king would add a change the task does not request.''',
'F03-O003':'''**Undēð** is present indicative third singular; the man opens the singular accusative sack. Its anomalous **dēð** stem must not be replaced by the infinitive undōn in a finite assertion.''',
'F03-O004':'''**Undydon** is preterite indicative third plural. Both subject and object are plural: **ðā męnn** opened **ðā saccas**. The preterite **dyde-** system is distinct from present undēð; keeping the singular object or present tense would miss part of the change.''',
'F03-O005':'''The king is the accusative singular object in both recognition sentences. The sack and sacks are accusative objects in the opening sentences. Subject person and number determine finite agreement, and the requested time determines the verb stem; object number determines its own article and noun. The nearer object does not govern the verb’s person or number.''',
'R02-O001':'''**Hiere** is genitive feminine singular, the daughter as possessor of **mōd**. **His** is genitive masculine singular referring to Apollonius; in the supplied love expression the affection is directed toward him. Thus “her heart fell in love with him,” or idiomatically “she fell in love with him.” It does not say that he fell in love with her.''',
'R02-O002':'''The princess is **ic**, the nominative giver and speaker of this new quotation. Apollonius is **ðē**, dative recipient of **giefe**, “I give you.” The preceding king’s speech has ended; keeping him as speaker would assign the gifts to the wrong person.''',
'R02-O003':'''In **ðe ðæt mǣden him forgiefen hæfde**, him is dative singular Apollonius, recipient of the slaves. In **and him cwæð tō**, him is dative plural, the slaves whom he now addresses; tō is postposed. The identical spellings have different numbers and referents, established by their clauses and the following plural commands.''',
'R03-O001':'''The subject of **geseah** is **ðæs cyninges dohtor**, the king’s daughter. The grammatical subject of **gefēoll** is singular **hiere mōd**, her heart or mind; hiere alone is a genitive possessor. Idiomatic English may say “she fell in love,” but that does not change the Old English subject’s structure.''',
'R03-O002':'''**Punda** is genitive plural of the counted weight-unit after **twā hund**. **Goldes** is genitive singular identifying the material measured. The supplied whole phrase means “two hundred pounds of gold.” Calling both simply “possessors” obscures the different dependencies; this task applies the visible quantity analysis rather than testing an unassigned numeral system.''',
'R03-O003':'''Original note13 expressly identifies **męnn** as the antecedent of **ðe**. The relative represents those men as the subject of **gesāwon**; **hīe** is accusative plural, the gifts they saw. **Giefa** is accusative plural object of **hęredon**, with genitive hiere naming the princess as their giver/possessor. Thus “all the men who saw them praised her gifts.” The intervening words do not make gifts the relative subject.''',
'R03-O004':'''In **bǣdon hīe gesunde bēon** (176.16), **gesunde** is accusative plural, agreeing with accusative **hīe**, the king and princess as subject of infinitive bēon. In **bēon gē gesunde** (176.18), it is nominative plural, agreeing with **gē** in a direct second-plural subjunctive wish. Both formulas mean “be well” or “farewell.” The adjective is not an adverb merely because English says “well”; the two bēon forms also have different grammatical functions.''',
'R04-O001':'''**Mid-ðȳ-ðe** introduces the temporal frame, “when the king’s daughter saw ….” Within it, **ðæt Apollonius … wæs getogen** is the content seen: his education and accomplishments. **Ðā gefēoll hiere mōd …** gives the ensuing main event. Geseah and wæs are indicative in this asserted circumstance. These are nested temporal and content relations followed by a main clause, not three interchangeable relative clauses.''',
'R04-O002':'''**Ðæt ic mōste giefan …** is the supplied permission-content construction: “you allowed me to give …,” as original note2 directs. Mōste expresses permission here, not obligation. **Swā-hwæt-swā** means “whatever,” the freely selected thing given: the daughter recalls what she **wolde**, and the king now says she may give what she **wille**. These are free-choice relatives, not direct questions. The earlier wolde is first-singular past subjunctive; wille is second-singular present subjunctive addressed to her.''',
'R04-O003':'''**Gān wē** is a first-plural present-subjunctive exhortation, “let us go.” Plain infinitive **sēcean** gives the action sought by going, finding their lodging. **Ðæt wē mægen ūs geręstan** gives its purpose, “so that we may rest”: mægen is first-plural present subjunctive, geręstan an infinitive, and **ūs** the accusative reflexive directed by original note177.2 (§184b). Neither the exhortation nor the purpose narrates an arrival or completed rest.''',
'R05-O001':'''She invokes her father’s earlier permission, then announces the gifts **be mīnes fæder lēafe**, with his permission. He confirms that she may give whatever she wishes. The daughter selects and enumerates the gifts; the king does not individually prescribe the stated amounts, clothing and slaves.''',
'R05-O002':'''He orders the slaves to take the gifts with them and proposes that they go to seek their lodging so that they can rest. The earlier guests have already gone home, but this last speech does not narrate Apollonius’s arrival, sleep or completed rest. “He plans/proposes to go and rest” is accurate; “he went to sleep” imports an event beyond the selected boundary.'''
}
